SwirlOS theme engine  -  /system/config/theme.json
==================================================

One JSON file controls how the desktop looks. Edit it with any text editor
(Settings > nothing to open: just save the file). Within about a second the
compositor re-reads it and the whole desktop and every open app re-theme, no
restart. Strict JSON: no comments, no trailing commas.

If the file has an error the WHOLE file is rejected, the previous look stays,
and a notification names the line ("line 14: window.corner_radius: expected a
number"). Unknown keys and out-of-range numbers are not errors: the key is
ignored / the number is clamped, and a warning notification tells you.
Delete the file to return to the built-in look.

Keys (defaults in brackets)
---------------------------
version            1
name               free text, up to 31 characters
base               "default" | "black" | "white"   the built-in palette that
                   every colour you do not set follows. Also the Settings >
                   Theme choice.

colors             "#RRGGBB" or "#RGB". A colour you leave out (or set to
                   null) follows the base palette. Keys you set stay put when
                   the base changes.
  accent
  window:  titlebar, titlebar_inactive, title_text, title_text_inactive,
           border, border_inactive, shadow,
           minimize, maximize, close, buttons_inactive,
           minimize_hover, maximize_hover, close_hover
  shell:   taskbar, panel, panel_border, panel_divider, field, button, hover,
           text, text_dim, text_faint
  app:     background, toolbar, card, field, border, divider, text, text_dim,
           text_faint, selection, danger, success, warning

window colours (all optional; the shipped file lists them as null)
  titlebar / titlebar_inactive         title bar fill, focused / other windows
  title_text / title_text_inactive     title text
  border                               the window outline (focused, and every
                                       window when border_inactive is unset)
  border_inactive                      outline of unfocused windows
  minimize / maximize / close          icon colour of that title-bar button on
                                       the focused window ("maximize" is the
                                       full-screen button)
  buttons_inactive                     icon colour of all three buttons on
                                       unfocused windows
  minimize_hover / maximize_hover /
  close_hover                          solid highlight drawn behind the button
                                       while the pointer is on it; the icon
                                       turns white or black, whichever is easier
                                       to read on that colour. Unset: the soft
                                       wash for minimize/maximize, red for close.
  Example - dark-red close highlight with a pale icon, gold border on the focused window:
    "window": { "close_hover": "#C42B1C", "close": "#FFB4A8",
                "border": "#E6B800", "border_inactive": "#555555" }
  (border thickness is window.border_width below; 0 hides the border)

font
  file             name of a .ttf in /system/fonts, "" = the boot font [""].
                   Must be FIXED-PITCH (the UI and terminal lay out on a
                   character grid). Bundled and usable: JetBrainsMono-Regular,
                   JetBrainsMono-Bold, monospace, monospace_bold. Proportional
                   fonts (sans_serif.ttf, serif.ttf ...) are refused with a
                   notification and the current font stays.
  title_size       10..20 [14]   window title text

window
  corner_radius    0..16 [8]
  border_width     0..6  [1]     pixels; the first one sits on the window edge,
                                 the rest grow outward. 0 = no border line.
  shadow.enabled   true | false  [true]
  shadow.size      0..40 [12]    how far the shadow reaches, px
  shadow.opacity   0..100 [32]   darkness at the window edge, percent

titlebar
  align            "left" | "center" | "right"  [left]   title text
  button_side      "right" | "left" [right]
  buttons          on-screen order, left to right, any of "minimize",
                   "maximize", "close", each at most once.
                   ["minimize","maximize","close"]. Leave "close" out and
                   windows can only be closed from the taskbar (a warning says so).
  icons            your own button pictures: put the full path of a .bmp in
                   "minimize", "maximize" and/or "close". "" = built-in glyph.
                     "icons": { "close": "/home/user/close.bmp" }
                   8, 24 or 32-bit BMP, any size up to 4096; it is drawn at its
                   own size when it fits 24x24, otherwise shrunk to fit. For a
                   see-through background use a 32-bit BMP with alpha, or paint
                   the background pure magenta (#FF00FF) = transparent.
                   Unfocused windows draw the icon dimmed; the hover highlight
                   (colors.window.*_hover, or the default wash) stays behind it.
                   After replacing a .bmp, save theme.json again to reload it.
                   A path that cannot be read shows a notification and keeps
                   the built-in glyph.

transparency       percent, 10..100 (100 = solid)
  active           window content, focused window [100]
  inactive         window content, other windows  [100]
  titlebar         title bar and border, all windows [100]
  rules            per-application overrides, up to 8:
                   [ { "type": "terminal", "active": 85, "inactive": 70 } ]
                   "active" / "inactive" are each optional.
                   types: terminal files about image_viewer text_editor
                   settings 3d_demo task_manager external calculator
                   driver_manager driver_properties browser paint installer
                   taskbar_toolbox

Settings app
------------
Accent colour, window radius, the shadow switch and the base palette are also
edited in Settings. Whatever the file states for those is kept in step: a
change made in Settings is written into theme.json in place (everything else
in the file, including layout and keys this version does not know, is left as
it is). A value the file does not state stays Settings-only.

Notes
-----
* Translucent windows blend over the wallpaper and the windows below. Each
  translucent window costs one extra blend pass over its area per repaint.
* Colours for a built-in palette that no key covers cannot be themed yet:
  the resize grip and the NetSurf page.
